  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Air is being pumped into a tire. The rate $r(t)$ at which air enters the tire is given by a function\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\frac{500}{1+2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},$ and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. Initially, there was $50\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ of air in the tire.\n\nWhich integral gives the volume of air in the tire $t$ minutes since the pumping started?",
  "worked_solution": "We're given the **rate of change of volume** $r(t).$ To find the **total volume** after $t$ minutes (call it $V(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{500}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.",
